WEBVTT - Trace directed cut (synthetic Kokoro af_heart, no autoplay)

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.960
Trace is a memory layer for language models. It organizes documents into schemas, so retrieval stays fast far beyond a context window.

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:15.049
Here, a real recorded run ingests eighty-eight documents, about twenty thousand tokens.

00:00:15.399 --> 00:00:26.471
Each document becomes small pieces called atoms: one hundred forty-four atoms, grouped under fifty-one schemas. The structure is real. The layout is drawn for readability.

00:00:26.821 --> 00:00:32.090
Now a query. Guided search follows the schema structure instead of scanning everything.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:41.315
Trace also repairs itself. In the measured ten-million-token run, it found and fixed every one of nearly six thousand integrity issues.

00:00:41.665 --> 00:00:54.379
That run processed over ten million tokens in about two hours and thirty-eight minutes, peaking near eight and a half gigabytes of memory. Guided recall measured point seven four. Leave-source-out, point four nine.

00:00:54.729 --> 00:01:05.097
This is a research preview. Reproducing the full ten-million run needs the original corpus, which is not yet published. The small run, you can try yourself today.
